A. Problem Description (by Advisees)
{Describe your team’s project title, high-level problem, and current solution. Include links to prototypes and screenshots if any.}


Children who undergo emergency surgery or are suddenly exposed to an unfamiliar healthcare environment can feel overwhelmed and anxious, especially when preparation and communication regarding procedures is limited. Parents of sick children are left to navigate difficult situations, trying to understand their child’s condition and make sense of the medical details well enough to relieve their worried child. 
One of the main challenges is the lack of accessible, child-friendly resources and the time needed to properly prepare children for surgery and recovery. While medical staff are responsible for providing quality patient care, their existing workloads can limit their capacity to adequately prepare children and support their post-operative recovery.
KareBEAR aims to address this gap by helping children and their caregivers better understand and navigate their medical recovery journeys through accessible, child-friendly education and post-operative care.
KareBEAR would use AI to adapt clinician-approved procedure and recovery instructions into short, child-friendly stories or comics featuring a bear companion. The content would explain what children may see, feel, and need to do, with additional explanations available for caregivers.
For the first prototype, we are considering one complete procedure or recovery scenario. The bear would present the information in short sections and ask a few questions to check understanding. A caregiver-assisted teach-back activity could invite children to explain an important instruction in their own words. Optional narration could support children with limited reading ability. Speech input, multilingual support, and generated video remain options to evaluate against our development timeline.
The application would provide educational support rather than medical advice. Essential instructions and warnings would need to remain consistent with the approved source material, and generated content would require review before use. Questions about symptoms, treatment decisions, or recovery outcomes would be directed to the care team. The bear would avoid promises such as “you’ll get better soon.




B. Technical Question (by Advisees)
{List at least 3 specific technical questions your project is facing.}
1. Given our course timeline, what features should we prioritize for a first prototype, and what tools would you recommend?
2. What do you believe would be a good balance between keeping the medical information accurate and also child-friendly/digestible?
3. Given the severity of certain health conditions, how can we develop a model that doesn’t border too closely between offering encouragement and false reassurance? For example, phrases like “you’ll get better soon” might do more harm to families than good. 


C. Recommendation (by Consultants)
{Each TCT member must enter 2-3 unique technical recommendations, with your name in brackets next to your bulleted items.}


[Sofiia] Q1. What to prioritize for the first prototype + tools?


Pick one common procedure (for example, getting an IV or a tonsil surgery) and build the full flow for it: upload a clinician-approved instruction, turn it into a short comic or story with the bear then ask 2–3 teach-back questions. One complete scenario will show the idea better than many half-finished ones. For the MVP, I would skip speech-to-text, because children’s speech is still hard for most engines to recognize reliable and testing that properly would take a lot of your time. Simple answer buttons with pictures work well for teach-back questions. Text-to-speech (the bear reading the story out loud) is much easier to add and still helps kids who can’t read yet. For generation, I would keep it for MVP, since it is the core idea of your app, but limit it to the story text and comic images ( generating a video can be more complex). 


[Sofiia]  Q2. Balance between medical accuracy and child-friendly language?


I had one idea in my mind, you can have 2 reading levels (for example, ages 3–6 with mostly pictures, and ages 7–12 with short sentences) instead of trying to make one version that works for everyone.


[Sofiia]  Q3. Encouragement without false reassurance?


I would suggest, focus on describing what will happen and how the child might feel, not on promising outcomes. For example, say “The doctors will take good care of you and it’s okay to feel scared” instead of “You’ll get better soon.” I’m not sure how good this suggestion is - but it’s one of the possible suggestions I had.


[Melvin] Q1. Make the stories ahead of time, not live.
 
I’d use AI as a writing tool for your team rather than something the child talks to. Generate the story for your one scenario with any major LLM (GPT, Claude or Gemini), fix anything that’s off, then save it as a JSON file that a simple React web app displays. Kids never see unchecked AI output, and you save time and tokens. For the bear, make a small set of poses once and reuse them, since generated images often change how the bear looks from panel to panel. For narration, the browser’s built-in text-to-speech works for free.
 
[Melvin] Q2. Keep the key facts separate from the story.
 
Before generating, pull the must-know instructions out of the clinician’s document into a short checklist (like when to stop eating, keeping the bandage dry, or telling a grown-up if it hurts more). The bear can explain them in its own words, but the checklist stays word for word in a small “for grown-ups” box. Also ask the AI to label which checklist item each part of the story covers, so you can quickly spot anything it left out.
 
[Melvin] Q3. Back up the prompt with a phrase filter.
 
Telling the model “don’t give false hope” won’t be enough on its own. Add a few good and bad example sentences to the prompt, plus a simple filter that flags promise-like phrases such as “you’ll get better soon,” “it won’t hurt,” or “everything will be fine.” Anything flagged gets rewritten or reviewed by a person. I’d especially ban “it won’t hurt,” because if it does hurt, kids stop trusting the bear.


[Ellen] R1. Consider how children’s varying literacy levels and speech patterns may affect their ability to interact with your application. The system should support age-appropriate explanations using simplified language, visual aids, and potentially voice-based interactions. If speech recognition is incorporated, evaluate its ability to accurately interpret children’s speech, particularly given differences in pronunciation, vocabulary, and speech development across age groups. However, to directly address your Q1, given the course’s time constraints, I recommend prioritizing touch-based interactions that have been shown to improve learning outcomes in children.


[Ellen] R2. I appreciate your emphasis on providing educational support rather than medical advice. To address your concerns in Q3 about potentially blurring the line between an educational tool and a therapy-like tool, I would recommend that your team first establish clear boundaries between these two purposes, perhaps by developing a codebook of rules outlining what the application should and should not do. Having these boundaries explicitly defined would not only help align your team’s understanding of the application’s intended purpose, but also guide the development of your AI system. For example, these rules could inform the system’s behaviour to ensure it focuses on helping children understand unfamiliar healthcare environments, procedures, and experiences, rather than providing diagnoses, treatment recommendations, or other forms of medical advice.




