Discussion
Care Bear’s Purpose Reducing Children’s Anxiety and Supporting Recovery
* Alexandra described Care Bear as a child-friendly educational app addressing anxiety around emergency surgery, unfamiliar healthcare environments, and recovery. Parents must understand medical instructions while reassuring their children, but clinicians’ workloads limit preparation and support. A nurse consulted by the team linked children’s comfort with procedures to recovery success. The proposed bear companion would explain experiences through interactive games, stories, or videos.
Primary Audience, Severity Levels, and Optional Parent Community
* The team clarified that children, rather than parents, are the primary audience for the MVP. Alexandra highlighted the challenge of making information useful yet simple enough for children while adapting to situations ranging from a prolonged emergency-room wait to pneumonia. A separate parent companion app or question-and-answer community was discussed as a possible extension, not the central prototype priority.
Clinician-Approved Content, AI Adaptation, and Teach-Back Safeguards
* Albert proposed transforming clinician-approved explanations into engaging cartoons, stories, and animations. Alexandra stressed that Care Bear should not provide medical advice; source material could include a doctor’s recovery plan or hospital documents. A proposed teach-back feature would ask children or caregivers to explain instructions in their own words to flag misunderstandings. The team stressed preserving important information and avoiding unapproved content.
Multilingual Voice Interaction and Children’s Speech Recognition
* Albert considered multilingual support important for children from diverse backgrounds, particularly before school age, and proposed voice feedback to bridge language differences. Melvin questioned speech engines’ ability to interpret users reliably. Albert viewed transcription accuracy as the main unresolved issue and cited approximately 98% accuracy for some engines, while acknowledging the need for research. Ellen recommended investigating benchmarks specifically covering children’s speech and vocabulary.


Four-Week Prototype Feasibility and Narrower MVP Scope
* Melvin questioned whether an app combining speech recognition and generated videos was feasible within four weeks. Albert said feature prioritization was already a question for the advisers. He proposed assembling a smaller prototype around one complete disease or operation scenario, potentially generating a comic rather than video to reduce time and token consumption, instead of covering many medical situations.
