E. Reflection on Consultation Process (by Consultants)
{As a TCT team, reflect on the consultation process: Which recommendations were you most confident about and least confident about? Why? What else would you like to find out about your client team’s problem if you had more time?}


[Sofiia] I felt most sure about skipping speech-to-text for the first prototype. Kids’ speech is pretty hard for these tools to understand and testing it properly would eat up a lot of their time. I was least sure about how to balance medical accuracy with simple language, since I’m not a medical person and don’t really know which details are the most important to keep. If I had more time, I’d be curious to find out how kids get prepared for surgery right now, like whether nurses just explain things to them or if there are books or videos. I’d also want to check if there are already any apps or tech out there doing something similar.


[Melvin] I was most confident about making the stories ahead of time instead of generating them live. It removes the biggest risk, an anxious kid seeing something wrong or scary, and it saves time too. I was least confident about the phrase filter, because false reassurance can be subtle (something like “this will be quick”), so a person still has to read everything. With more time, I’d want to know who would actually review the stories in a real hospital, since the whole problem is that staff are already short on time.


[Ellen] Ultimately, you guys did a great job presenting your problem statement and idea, and I can really see the benefit and need for something like this in the community. Although most of my recommendations were centred around your team’s need for a stronger implementation plan to personalize your application to children, this at the same time shows you guys are headed in the right direction, as rather than having to broad of a focus on the general low-health literacy population, having children as your target patient audience significantly narrows down your project’s focus to specific behaviour, research methods and frameworks to follow, and datasets to use.
